# G115 — Mind Mapper

*v2. See `mind-mapper.md` for prose: radial polar-to-cartesian layout,
parent→children tree (not flat list), per-branch color inheritance.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Idea Map

[idea-map @root-tag @cx @cy
    IN ORDER TO
        render vault ideas tagged under @root-tag as a radial mind map
        with branches inheriting the parent's color

    @tree <- [vault[build-tag-tree {root-tag: @root-tag}]]
    @nodes <- [mm[radial-layout {tree: @tree,
                                  cx: @cx, cy: @cy,
                                  radius-step: 150}]]

    for @node in @nodes
        [canvas[draw-node {node: @node}]]
        for @child in @node:children
            [canvas[draw-edge {from: @node, to: @child,
                                color: @node:color}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[mm
    node          {id, label}
    with-child    {parent, child}            ;; parent gains child
    with-color    {node, color}
    radial-layout {tree, cx, cy, radius-step}
    subtree-size  {node}                     ;; counts transitive descendants
]

[canvas
    draw-node {node}
    draw-edge {from, to, color}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: polar-to-cartesian as resolver internal

The v1 spec exposed `polar_to_cartesian(cx, cy, angle, radius)` as a
public resolver native. In Innate this is internal math — the
choreographer asks for `radial-layout`, the resolver handles the
polar-to-cartesian conversion. The user sees geometry, not math.

[mm[radial-layout {tree: @t, cx: 500, cy: 500, radius-step: 120}
    where
        level-0 sits at the center
        level-1 distributes around the full circle
        level-2 distributes within its parent's arc
        each level sits at radius = depth × radius-step
]

The `where` clauses specify the *layout contract* — how angles and
radii should be assigned — without prescribing the trigonometric
implementation.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @root <- [mm[node {id: 0, label: "Rosetta Stone"}]]
    @c1   <- [mm[node {id: 1, label: "Graphics"}]]
    @c2   <- [mm[node {id: 2, label: "Text"}]]
    @c3   <- [mm[node {id: 3, label: "Classes"}]]
    @root <- [mm[with-child {parent: @root, child: @c1}]]
    @root <- [mm[with-child {parent: @root, child: @c2}]]
    @root <- [mm[with-child {parent: @root, child: @c3}]]

    @nodes <- [mm[radial-layout {tree: @root,
                                   cx: 500, cy: 500, radius-step: 100}]]
    -> @nodes count        ;; -> 4
    -> @root:subtree-size  ;; -> 3 (children, not self)
]
